roves"}}, {"t": 492, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " build"}}, {"t": 492, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " efficiency"}}, {"t": 494, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": ","}}, {"t": 494, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " and"}}, {"t": 494, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " allows"}}, {"t": 495, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " parallel"}}, {"t": 497, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " execution"}}, {"t": 498, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " of"}}, {"t": 501, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " build"}}, {"t": 501, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " steps"}}, {"t": 502, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "."}}, {"t": 502, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": " ["}}, {"t": 504, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "docker"}}, {"t": 505, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": ":"}}, {"t": 506, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "building"}}, {"t": 507, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "/m"}}, {"t": 507, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "ulti"}}, {"t": 509, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "-stage"}}, {"t": 510, "type": "llm.token", "data": {"delta": "]"}}, {"t": 514, "type": "gate.citations", "data": {"passed": true, "cited": ["docker:building/multi-stage"], "invented": [], "said_not_in_docs": false}}, {"t": 514, "type": "answer", "data": {"text": "A multi\u2011stage build is a Dockerfile that uses multiple `FROM` statements to create separate build stages. Each stage can use a different base image, and artifacts can be selectively copied from one stage to another, leaving only the necessary files in the final image. This approach reduces image size, improves build efficiency, and allows parallel execution of build steps. [docker:building/multi-stage]", "citations": [{"n": 1, "tag": "docker:building/multi-stage", "source": "docker", "doc": "building/multi-stage.md", "lines": "L1-21", "passages": ["Multi-stage builds are useful to anyone who has struggled to optimize\nDockerfiles while keeping them easy to read and maintain.\n\n## Use multi-stage builds\n\nWith multi-stage builds, you use multiple `FROM` statements in your Dockerfile.\nEach `FROM` instruction can use a different base, and each of them begins a new\nstage of the build. You can selectively copy artifacts from one stage to\nanother, leaving behind everything you don't want in the final image.\n\nThe following Dockerfile has two separate stages: one for building a binary,\nand another where the binary gets copied from the first stage into the next stage.\n\n```dockerfile\n# syntax=docker/dockerfile:1\nFROM golang:\nWORKDIR /src\nCOPY <<EOF ./main.go\npackage main\n\nimport \"fmt\""], "url": "https://github.com/docker/docs/blob/ff96ad1711065cf2e9c3f1d701dad04775834f70/content/manuals/build/building/multi-stage.md"}]}}, {"t": 514, "type": "done", "data": {"ms_total": 513.8, "llm_ms": 465.0, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 209, "prompt_tokens": 1090, "total_tokens": 1299, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 121}, "queue_time": 0.063372521, "prompt_time": 0.055022256, "completion_time": 0.260032445, "total_time": 0.315054701}, "ratelimit": {"limit-requests": "1000", "limit-tokens": "8000", "remaining-requests": "990", "remaining-tokens": "3099", "reset-requests": "14m24s", "reset-tokens": "36.757s"}}}]}